<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">ambush</ansi>

An <ansi fg="magenta-bold">ambush</ansi> is what you get when you attack while hidden. The
combat text marks it as a <ansi fg="magenta-bold">surprise attack</ansi>. There is no command to
type. You hide first, then attack or shoot, and your opening blow
becomes the ambush on its own.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Setting One Up ━━━</ansi>

  1. Use <ansi fg="command">sneak</ansi> to slip out of sight.
  2. Check that your special move has come back, with <ansi fg="command">cooldowns</ansi>.
  3. <ansi fg="command">attack <target></ansi> to open in melee, or <ansi fg="command">shoot <target></ansi> to open
     from cover.

Requires the <ansi fg="skill">skullduggery</ansi> skill, which is what hides you in the first place.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Only the First Blow ━━━</ansi>

An ambush is one strike, not a whole round of them. Anything else you
swing in that round lands as ordinary fighting. Only the opening blow is
marked in the combat text, so you can tell which one it was. The opener
is spent even if it misses, so you get one attempt.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ It Can Be Answered ━━━</ansi>

An ambush is not a free hit. A ready defender may dodge, parry or block
it, just as with any other attack, and the game names what they did. An
ambushing shot can be dodged or blocked, but not parried, since there is
no blade coming for them to turn aside.

If they stop it, you lose the advantage, and the blow lands as an
ordinary one or not at all. If it gets through cleanly it strikes as a
critical hit, and it bites deeper the better your <ansi fg="skill">skullduggery</ansi>. A
practised thief opens a fight far more sharply than a beginner does.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ You Are Seen ━━━</ansi>

Striking gives your hiding place away at once, whether you hit or miss.
You cannot fight on from the shadows, and a shot from cover reveals you
the same way. Plan the rest of the fight as an open one.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ It Spends Your Special Move ━━━</ansi>

The ambush uses the shared special move cooldown, so it competes with
<ansi fg="command">bash</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">trip</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">kick</ansi> and the rest. If the cooldown has not
recovered you get no ambush. Your attack simply goes in as an ordinary
one, and you are told so.

Archers should watch this closely. <ansi fg="command">reload</ansi> spends the same recovery.
Reloading and then hiding often leaves you with nothing ready and an
ordinary shot instead. Load first, then hide, and let the cooldown
return before you fire.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Shooting Into the Next Room ━━━</ansi>

Only a shot at something in your own room can be an ambush. Firing into
an adjacent room is an ordinary shot however well hidden you are. It
gains nothing from stealth, but it does not give your hiding place away
either, so you keep your cover.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Shooting Without Being Bothered ━━━</ansi>

This is a separate matter from the ambush, and it matters just as much.
A ranged weapon is far more dangerous when nothing in the room is
attacking you. You cannot steady an aimed shot while you are fending
someone off, and the shot suffers badly for it.

That is the archer's whole problem, and the answer is always the same:
do not be the one being hit. Bring a companion or a party member to hold
their attention, or put a room between you and them and fire from there.

